EU icons for labelling AI-generated content
============================================

The twelve SVG files in assets/ are the European Commission's "EU icons for labelling
AI-generated content", published on 2026-06-10 as Annex 1 of the Code of Practice on
Transparency of AI-generated Content (Article 50 of the AI Act). They are third-party data
inside this GPL-3.0 application, not part of its licence.

Source
  https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/eu-icons-labelling-ai-generated-content
  SVG pack: https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/dae/redirection/document/129546
  Code of Practice: https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/dae/redirection/document/129555

Terms, quoted from the source page (read 2026-09-12)
  "These icons are made publicly available for everyone to use freely, without the need for
  attribution to the Commission or the AI Office."
  "However, signatories of the code of practice should use the icon in accordance with its
  placement specifications."
  "Usage of these icons by non-signatories of the code should not be construed as signaling
  of their adherence to the code."

  Code of Practice, Measure 1.1: "The letters in the acronym will have the same vertical
  dimension. If resized, the proportions of the letters must be preserved." The icon "may
  be expressed in different styles (e.g., contrast ratio, colour, or typography), as long as
  it remains clear, accessible, and distinguishable".

What that means here
  The files are unchanged apart from their names (LABEL_AI GENERATED_black transparent.svg
  became ai-generated_black_transparent.svg, and so on). The plugin only rasterises them at
  the size the user asks for, keeping the proportions. The label is a disclosure mark: adding
  it says that the picture was generated or modified by AI, nothing more. It does not certify
  compliance with the AI Act or adherence to the Code of Practice.

  No named licence (CC BY, CC0) accompanies the icons, and the Commission's general legal
  notice excludes "trade marks, registered designs, logos and names" from its CC BY 4.0
  default without saying whether the icons fall under that exclusion. The statement above is
  what the publisher says; if you redistribute the files, keep this notice with them.
